//! Power rails.

/// A DC power rail: two terminals and the voltage between them. A rail on a board that
/// nothing on the board drives is supplied from outside it.
///
/// `current` is what whatever is behind the rail draws from the net through `pos`: positive
/// when it draws, negative when it supplies. A design states the load side
/// (`.output.current &= 0mA..50mA`); the checker adds up the rails on each net so that
/// what is drawn equals what is supplied (ADR 0007).
interface Rail {
    .pos: Pin
    .neg: Pin
    .voltage: Voltage
    .current: Current
    /// The highest voltage the rail reaches, transients included (a clamp's voltage, a load
    /// dump). `voltage` is the steady range. Rails joined with `~` share it, and a part that
    /// limits a transient states it for the rail behind it.
    .peak: Voltage
    /// The highest voltage the rail reaches the wrong way round, as a positive number: the
    /// negative transient of a vehicle's supply, or what a miswired harness puts on it (the
    /// check treats it as a voltage that must be blocked). A design states it for the input,
    /// where it can happen, and the first part of a `~>` chain from the rail that has a
    /// `.supply_reverse_peak` is checked against it (ADR 0091). Left unstated, nothing is
    /// checked and nothing is listed as not evaluated: the member is `optional` (ADR 0074),
    /// because a member that is not would list one more note for every diode or TVS diode in
    /// a chain from any rail, such as the rail behind a diode, which has no reverse peak and
    /// needs none. It is a positive number (a datasheet says `-100V`), so that every
    /// rating reads `<=` in the same direction as `.peak`; the number has the opposite sign to
    /// the voltage it is. Only the first part of the chain that has the field is given it:
    /// the part that meets the reversed supply blocks it or conducts it, and what is behind
    /// is protected by it (a TVS diode behind a blocking diode would be found to conduct a
    /// transient it never sees). A rail behind a blocking diode states `.reverse_peak &= 0V`
    /// itself, since nothing derives it. The reverse voltage a diode sees is the supply's
    /// reverse peak plus whatever voltage the rail behind it holds (a capacitor charged to the
    /// input's voltage), which is not added: a board states `.d.max_reverse_voltage >=
    /// .supply.reverse_peak + .load.peak`.
    .reverse_peak: Voltage optional
    /// The narrowest track the nets of this rail are routed with: a lower bound, written as
    /// a range (`.min_track &= 0.5mm..1m`). Both pins get it, since the return current is the
    /// same current. A net takes the widest of the rails on it, so `GND` is as wide as the
    /// widest rail that returns to it (ADR 0020). Unset, the net is routed at the process's
    /// least.
    .min_track: Length
}
